WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.511
Hello!

2
00:00:00.511 --> 00:00:06.902
Today we look at equations in which the derivative is not given explicitly,

3
00:00:06.902 --> 00:00:10.310
for example y prime squared equals four.

4
00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:16.445
For such an equation several integral curves can pass through one point.

5
00:00:16.445 --> 00:00:24.710
By the end you will know two techniques: solving for the derivative, and introducing a parameter.

6
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.922
Denote the derivative by p.

7
00:00:27.922 --> 00:00:32.749
The equation reads F of x, y, p equals zero.

8
00:00:32.749 --> 00:00:40.208
If F is a polynomial in p, its degree is the degree of the equation.

9
00:00:40.208 --> 00:00:48.326
If p has several roots at a point, each root gives its own integral curve.

10
00:00:48.576 --> 00:00:56.895
Example: y prime squared, minus x plus y times y prime, plus x y, equals zero.

11
00:00:56.895 --> 00:01:04.361
This is a quadratic in p, and it factors as p minus x times p minus y.

12
00:01:04.361 --> 00:01:13.214
So the equation splits into two simple ones: y prime equals x, or y prime equals y.

13
00:01:13.464 --> 00:01:18.103
From the first, y equals one half x squared plus C.

14
00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:22.196
From the second, y equals C times e to the x.

15
00:01:22.196 --> 00:01:26.834
The general integral is the union of both families.

16
00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:34.838
Check: in the first family p equals x, in the second p equals y, so the product is zero.

17
00:01:35.088 --> 00:01:41.231
If solving for the derivative is hard, take p as a parameter.

18
00:01:41.231 --> 00:01:44.957
For example, x equals p cubed plus p.

19
00:01:44.957 --> 00:01:52.611
Then dx equals three p squared plus one, times dp, and dy equals p times dx.

20
00:01:52.611 --> 00:02:01.070
Integrating, y equals three quarters p to the fourth plus one half p squared plus C.

21
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.106
The answer is parametric.

22
00:02:04.106 --> 00:02:12.242
Check: dividing dy by dp by dx by dp gives p, so y prime really equals p.

23
00:02:12.242 --> 00:02:19.598
Remember the key formula: dy equals p dx, not the other way round.

24
00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:31.078
The graph shows three curves of the family y equals one half x squared and two curves of the family y equals C e to the x.

25
00:02:31.078 --> 00:02:37.982
They touch each other on the line y equals x, where the two roots coincide.

26
00:02:38.232 --> 00:02:40.375
Now check yourself.

27
00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:44.774
Read the question and choose an answer.
